    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Доброе утро, Виктор.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good morning, Viktor.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'good' straight to 'Viktor'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Доброе утро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good morning.' Notice the word order: 'good' comes first, and 'morning' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Виктор приготовил такой омлет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Victor prepared such an omelet.' — Notice the proper name meaning 'Viktor' paired with the verb for 'cooked'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Это типа извинения?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Is this a kind of apology?' The first content word means 'this', and 'apologies' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это типа омлет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is a kind of omelet.' The key word here means 'this', which Viktor combines with 'guy'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ешь давай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come on, eat.' In Russian, 'eat' leads into 'let's' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я опоздаю на работу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will be late for work.' Viktor starts with 'I' and pairs it with 'to be late' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Да, кушайте быстрее.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, eat faster.' the speaker puts 'eat' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'faster'. And Lyova jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Ладно, я съем это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alright, I will eat this.' The key word here means 'I', which Lyova combines with 'to eat up'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это очень вкусно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is very delicious.' A compact expression built around 'this' and 'it's tasty'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Нам пора идти.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It is time for us to go.' Listen for the word meaning 'us' right alongside 'it's time' in this line. Lyova answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Спасибо за завтрак.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you for the breakfast.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'thank you' straight to 'breakfast'. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
